   "title": "Open-world terrain tiles (read me first)",
   "properties": {
    "text": ""
   },
   "widgets_values": [
    "A 2 km × 2 km open-world terrain delivered as 4 × 4 tiles, engine-agnostic:\n  · Height: 1025² samples, 2 m per cell; each tile a 257² 16-bit PNG, **neighbouring tiles share the row of pixels on their common edge** (heights at the seams are bit-identical when stitched); metres = minimum + value × range, the range is in Height.json's range_scaled (one range for the whole map, the same for all tiles). A full-map .r16 is included too.\n  · Weight maps: six layers Grass / ForestFloor / Sediment / Rock / Snow / Water, one 8-bit greyscale PNG per tile per layer, **summing to 1 everywhere** (usable directly as the engine's terrain layers).\n  · Instances: trees (3 pine and 2 broadleaf kinds, split by altitude), bushes, rocks (asset names match the SM_Rock_0N exported by the rock pipeline), written as CSV in 512 m tiles, plus Instances_index.json listing each tile.\n\nCoordinate convention (written into every .json): origin at the north-west corner = top left of the height map, x east, z south (matching image row order), y up, units in metres. Instance transforms are read the same way as Copy to Points, so what the preview places is what the table says.\n\n① Terrain: fractal noise + mountain belt → Stream Power cuts river valleys → thermal erosion → hydraulic erosion (one minute on numpy, over ten seconds with CUDA) → flow accumulation → Splat Map.\n② Ecology: forest = grassland × below the tree line × gentle slope × noise patches, then split into six weight layers.\n③ Distribution: each of the three instance kinds scatters by its density map, samples height, picks an asset and sets orientation (trees upright with random rotation, rocks tilted a bit towards the slope).\n④ Write files + preview (full-map mesh + instance proxies)."
   ],
   "color": "#432",
   "bgcolor": "#653"
  },
